0..73 Comment "/// A lambda over a composite path, against the composite of two lambdas."
74..77 Comment "///"
78..140 Comment "/// `q ↦ Path.step(Path.step(q, g), h)` and the composite of"
141..224 Comment "/// `q ↦ Path.step(q, g)` and `r ↦ Path.step(r, h)` are one function. Each body"
225..301 Comment "/// agrees with the other once the machine takes the bind at the endpoint of"
302..324 Comment "/// `Path.step(q, g)`."
325..328 Comment "///"
329..409 Comment "/// The composite\'s body binds `Path.step(q, g)`, a transport stuck on `g`, into"
410..490 Comment "/// `t ↦ Path.step(t, h)`. That transport returns the value it moves, whatever"
491..568 Comment "/// its path. So the bind is its continuation at that endpoint, the transport"
569..644 Comment "/// along `h` that the lambda writes. Inside an action\'s motive, the kernel"
645..719 Comment "/// reads the two codes by its own rules, which run the composite\'s calls."
720..723 Comment "///"
724..796 Comment "/// - see: #3824 — a call of a returning variable held by the same law"
797..875 Comment "/// - see: #4011 — the checker refused the composite against its two lambdas"
876..956 Comment "/// - see: #4005 — the action over the composite, which the code walk hands on"
958..996 Comment "/// The composite of `F` and then `G`."
997..1000 Keyword "def"
